the city of dubrovnik on croatia's  adriatic coast is  often called the jewel of the  mediterranean it's famous for its  medieval buildings and beautiful narrow  alleyways  the pozod restaurant is situated right  at the edge of the historic district  a favorite of tourists and locals alike  it was opened by mario schabel  six years ago since then it's turned  into one of the city's top eateries  having a restaurant is more more than  job for me  that's a life you need to live with this  restaurant if you  that's like a wife this is my second  wife  fish and seafood are typical of  dubrovnik's cuisine  and the dalmatian region as a whole  local fishermen deliver their catch to  the restaurant each morning  one of its specialties is mussels  bizarra a regional dish made of  shellfish  the muscles what we get in our  restaurant is coming  from the dominican coast and it's so  fresh and it's  most important thing the people do that  just for the living and  the delivery us over here every morning  to get  fresh things in our restaurant  but there's more to mussels bizarro  mario heads to the market in dubrovnik's  historic district  which is open every weekday  is one of croatia's most important  agricultural regions  so there's a rich selection  mario buys locally grown vegetables and  herbs for his restaurant  they'll also go into the sauce for his  muscles bazara  this is the best place in the brony in  an old town because  you can find the fresh ingredients for  the restaurant and a buy on the same  place  they're coming all from the jupa that's  the near of the bronic  after shopping it's time to return to  the restaurant chef rajon knezich is  cooking today  and the ingredients are ready to go in  addition to mussels  there's also tomatoes onions garlic  parsley  olive oil and white wine  first the vegetables are chopped to  shorten the cooking time  while still retaining much of the flavor  and after that's ready it's time to  prepare the mussels  i put them in a pan with some hot olive  oil you don't need to add water  the mussels already have sea water in  them  after a few minutes on high heat the  shells open  that's a sign that the mussels are ready  now it's time to saute onions and garlic  for the sauce  next add tomatoes and parsley  finally the mussels are added to the  sauce and it's all quenched with white  wine  we're using a dry white wine it boils  very briefly  so the alcohol evaporates but the flavor  remains  now they're ready for serving once  considered a humble dish  mussel's bizarre is a relative newcomer  on the menus at finer restaurants  the people probably before 100 or 200  years  eat the muscles buzara because they  don't have enough  nice white fish and probably nice  quality of  meat and they are preparing probably the  buzara  for the for the lunch for a huge family  and they are probably use more bread  than than mussels  nowadays mussels bizarra are considered  a delicacy  but they're still served with bread and  they're supposedly best washed down with  a dry  white wine a local one of course
 

